    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Corbin Carroll** and **Isaac Paredes** anchoring the lineup, both boasting scores above 72. **Corbin Carroll** is our premier play; while he faces a left-handed arm in Wrobleski, his elite sprint speed is boosted by the pitcher's slow delivery, creating a high-probability environment for stolen bases that offsets the platoon disadvantage. Similarly, **Isaac Paredes** benefits from a high-efficiency matchup against V\u00e1squez, where his strong platoon split against right-handers and favorable position in the three-hole drive his value. **Mickey Moniak** also emerges as a high-upside play (71.00) due to a massive +14% platoon edge against a hittable 4.50 xERA arm, making him a critical source of counting stats today.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved keeping **Cole Young** on the bench. He finished with a 42.58 efficiency score, narrowly missing the cut behind **Travis Bazzana** and **Austin Riley**. While **Cole Young** offers excellent utility with his speed-to-delivery profile against Seymour, his overall base projection couldn't overcome the negative park and SP skill factors inherent in his specific matchup. Meanwhile, the lineup is heavily exposed to the E. Miller matchup, where **Max Muncy**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Henry Bolte** are all playing. While the +7% park factor and potential wind-aided home runs provide upside, there is inherent risk in stacking three hitters against a starter with a respectable 3.53 xERA. We are currently navigating the absences of **James Wood**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, and **Giancarlo Stanton**, which compresses our depth and forces us to lean on the efficiency of our fringe OF options like **Zach McKinstry** to maintain our competitive pace.",
